course: dictionary
lesson: 12444
title: Decorative: pretty, not just useful
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Decorative means used to make something look pretty or attractive, rather than to be useful. «The plate has a decorative border of blue flowers.» The border is there so the plate looks nice, not so that food sits better on it.
explain: Decorate is the verb, meaning to add pretty things to something. Decorative is the adjective, and it describes things that are made for looks. When you meet decorative, ask yourself: what job does this thing do? If the answer is looking good, it is decorative.
explain: Decorative can also describe something that looks nice but does little work. «The old vase is purely decorative; you cannot put flowers in it.» Purely means only, so the vase is just for looks. Here the context clue is the word «cannot»: a useful vase would hold flowers.
fact: Decorative means used to make something look pretty or attractive, rather than to be useful.
fact: The verb decorate means to add pretty things to something.
fact: «Purely decorative» means only for looks, with no real use.
fact: Something that looks nice but does little work can also be called decorative.
fact: Common phrases: «decorative border», «decorative lights», «decorative arts».
exercise: «Mum hung decorative lights around the garden for the party.» What does decorative mean here? | Made of wood | Used to light the way so people can see | Heavy and hard to move | *Used to make the garden look pretty
exercise: «The old vase is purely decorative.» What does purely decorative mean? | Made of glass only | *Only for looks, with no real use | Very large and heavy | Good for holding flowers
exercise: Which thing is most likely decorative? | A kettle | *A string of bright fairy lights on a tree | A tap that brings water | A bus timetable
test: Priya says decorative means useful. Is she right? | *No, decorative means made to look pretty rather than to be useful | Yes, decorative means useful for cooking | No, decorative means very small | Yes, decorative means strong and sturdy
test: «The cushions on the sofa are decorative.» Why are they decorative? | They keep you warm at night | They keep the rain out | *They are there to look nice, not for a job | They are heavy to lift
exam: Which sentence uses decorative correctly? | The decorative spade digs holes in the garden | The decorative bus takes us to school | *The gold frame around the picture is decorative, just to look pretty | The fridge is decorative because it keeps food cold
